Thanks Simon,

you are right. I was not aware of REXP and thought Rserve can only  
send objects to the client that can be assigned to Java objects, like  
int, double, arrays etc.

But there is still a problem. It's not really an Rserve problem, but  
related to it. Maybe you can give me another hint.

Transfering R-objects is not the problem anymore, as long as I have  
the REXP-object (i.e. do  eval and assign directly one after the  
other). But in my case I have to write this object to a file and  
later have to read it back into Java and subsequently transfer it to  
Rserve. What I do is:

// Get R-object
REXP testObj = c.eval("R.object");
byte [] testObjByte = testObj.asBytes();

// Write to file
File f = new File("path/to/somewhere.dat");
ObjectOutputStream out = new ObjectOutputStream( new FileOutputStream 
(f) );
out.writeObject(testObjByte);
out.close();

// Read back
ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream( new FileInputStream(f) );
Object testObjBack = in.readObject();
in.close();

Now I've got an Java-Object. But how do I get it back into Rserve?  
There's no possibility to assign a Java-Object to an R-object, right?  
What can I do to solve this?


Christian

Hi Simon,

> You just cast it - that's all:
> REXP r = (REXP) in.readObject();
I already tried that, but got a ClassCastException.

> Note, however, that not all types are supported in assigning. If  
> you have problems, do the serialization on R side, get the raw  
> bytes from the resulting REXP and store that instead.
I also did the serialization in R before calling the object. Like that:

c.voidEval( "R.object <- serialize(R.object.unserialized, NULL)" );

Now I changed my code to the following:

// Get R-object
REXP testObj = c.eval("R.object");
byte [] testObjByte = testObj.asBytes();

// Write to file
File f = new File("path/to/somewhere.dat");
FileOutputStream out = new FileOutputStream(f);
out.write(testObjByte);
out.close();

// Read file
FileInputStream in new FileInputStream(f);
byte [] backByte = new byte [ (int) f.length() ];
in.read(backByte);
in.close();

REXP back = (REXP) new REXPRaw(backByte);
c.assign( "backTestObj", back );

Further processing of the R-object works properly. Seems to be  
solved, then...

Thanks again,
Christian
